From: Project Luma <builds@project-luma.local>
Subject: [PATCH] luma: stop the lock screen clock from wedging the unlock

A successful password unlock could leave the screen spinning forever.
The journal shows PAM authenticating, then gnome-shell throwing
"GnomeDesktop.WallClock has been already disposed" from
ScreenShield._completeDeactivate(). The lock screen clock disposed its
WallClock from one destroy handler while the signal tracker was still
holding signals connected against the same widget; the loser of that
race threw, and the exception aborted the rest of the deactivation
before _setLocked(false) ever ran. Cancelling the prompt and retrying
worked because the dialog was already gone the second time.

diff --git a/js/ui/prairieLogin.js b/js/ui/prairieLogin.js
--- a/js/ui/prairieLogin.js
+++ b/js/ui/prairieLogin.js
@@ -33,7 +33,19 @@
         this._wallClock.connectObject(
             'notify::clock', this._updateClock.bind(this), this);
         this._updateClock();
-        this.connect('destroy', () => this._wallClock.run_dispose());
+        // connectObject registered the signals above against this widget, so
+        // the signal tracker disconnects them when it is destroyed. Disposing
+        // the clock from a second destroy handler raced that cleanup, and
+        // whichever ran last found the object already gone. The exception it
+        // threw escaped into the middle of ScreenShield._completeDeactivate(),
+        // which then never reached _setLocked(false) — so a *successful*
+        // password unlock left the shield up and the screen appeared to load
+        // forever, until the prompt was cancelled and retried. Dropping the
+        // tracked signals before disposing makes both orders equivalent.
+        this.connect('destroy', () => {
+            this._wallClock.disconnectObject(this);
+            this._wallClock.run_dispose();
+        });
     }
 
     _updateClock() {
